---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Button from "../components/Button.astro";

const readers = [
  ["Mail and calendar", "Gmail, Outlook, IMAP, Google Calendar"],
  ["Files", "Google Drive, OneDrive"],
  ["Chat and meetings", "Slack, Teams, Zoom"],
  ["Project tools", "Jira, Asana, Linear, Notion"],
  ["CRM and finance", "Salesforce, HubSpot, Stripe, QuickBooks, DocuSign"],
  ["Databases", "Airtable; Postgres and MySQL are declared, with drivers still to wire"],
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Deployment. Functionary"
  description="Where Functionary runs, what leaves the machine, and what is not yet available: no packaged build, no hosted service, licensing undecided."
>
  <section class="section first">
    <div class="container measure">
      <h1>Where it runs, and what leaves.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Functionary is built to run on a machine you control. Today the
        repository runs locally for development. There is no packaged build,
        no hosted service, and the license is not yet decided. What follows is
        the shape of the thing, not an install guide.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container measure">
      <h2>A flow is a source file in a project.</h2>
      <p class="stack-p">
        Each flow is one file in a fixed subset of Rust, kept with its project.
        The canvas is drawn from it and edits are written back to it. Every
        save is an immutable revision with a content hash, and a run binds to
        the exact revision it executed. You can read the source, but the
        product is built so that you never have to.
      </p>
      <p>
        Run history, receipts, run tables and the knowledge base live under one
        data directory. Canvas positions, titles and colours are layout, kept
        separately from the flow's meaning.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container measure">
      <h2>Egress is a list of steps you can read.</h2>
      <p class="stack-p">
        Nothing leaves the machine except through a step on the canvas. A
        Source fetches a URL or reads from a connected system. A block calls
        the model you connected to it. A Write puts a value somewhere. Each
        of those is a card you can see before you run, and each leaves a
        receipt after.
      </p>
      <p class="stack-p">
        Test withholds the writes. It does not withhold the reads or the model
        calls, because a dry run that skips them would not tell you anything.
        If a step must not reach a remote model, it says so, and a value
        labelled as local is refused at that step before it is sent.
      </p>
      <p>
        The agents inside blocks run on one vetted model through a provider
        configured to retain nothing. Telemetry export is off unless you
        configure an endpoint.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>Read from the systems you already have.</h2>
        <p class="stack-p">
          A Source can read from twenty-one systems. The readers are read-only
          and tested against recorded responses. Writing back to these systems
          is decided and not yet built.
        </p>
      </div>
      <dl class="readers">
        {readers.map(([k, v]) => (
          <div>
            <dt>{k}</dt>
            <dd>{v}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container measure">
      <h2>One person, or an organization.</h2>
      <p class="stack-p">
        A personal workspace has one person who creates, edits, runs and
        reviews, with no roles. A governed workspace names editors, runners,
        reviewers and administrators, decides whether agents may Run as well
        as Test, and will be able to require an approved revision before an
        unattended run once that gate is wired in. Organization rules only constrain a setting; they never supply
        its value.
      </p>
      <p>
        Agents hold their own credentials. An agent is never a flow's owner,
        never deletes one, and never approves a revision.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section cta">
    <div class="container measure">
      <h2>Not yet available.</h2>
      <p class="stack-p">
        The new backend is on an integration branch and has not landed. When
        it does, this page will say how to run it and under what terms.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <Button href="/how-it-works" variant="primary">See one job end to end</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .first {
    border-top: none;
  }
  h1 {
    max-width: 22ch;
  }
  .lead {
    margin-top: var(--s-24);
  }
  h2 {
    max-width: 26ch;
  }
  .stack-p {
    margin-bottom: var(--s-16);
  }
  .section p {
    margin-top: var(--s-16);
  }
  .readers {
    margin-top: var(--s-32);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-20);
  }
  .readers dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-12);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--fg-0);
  }
  .readers dd {
    margin: var(--s-4) 0 0;
    color: var(--fg-2);
    font-size: var(--ms-small);
  }
  .actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--s-12);
    margin-top: var(--s-32);
  }
  .cta {
    background: var(--bg-1);
  }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .readers {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
